in this episode of cloud performance  atlas we help some ml video experts with  their download speeds will we hit the  target bitrate stay tuned to find out  video miner is a video processing and ml  company and they came to me with an  issue it was taking about two minutes to  transfer a video from cloud storage to a  VM where computation could occur now 120  seconds may not sound like a long time  to transfer a four gig video but when  you're paying for the CPU while that's  downloading it feels like an eternity  worse off is that one of their engineers  was reporting that curl was  outperforming gsutil for the Downloads  which didn't sound right to me  so of course I wanted to see the problem  myself so I set up a small test that  downloaded videos of different sizes  from a cloud storage bucket to a VM in  u.s. central one and actually confirmed  their performance findings asking around  it seemed as though the challenge with  gsutil  is that the default settings don't line  up well for large file downloads by  default gsutil were spread downloads  across four threads but only use one  process for copying a single file on a  powerful VM we can improve that  performance by limiting the number of  threads forcing the system to use  multiple processes instead to do this we  can specify a command line option to  reduce the thread count to one now with  this small change we improve the  performance but we're still not at the  level that curl is showing when fetching  through HTTP so for large files there is  a nifty feature in GS util that should  help us out a bit more GS util uses HTTP  range get requests to perform sliced  downloads in parallel when downloading  large objects from Google Cloud Storage  along with setting the thread count to 1  we will also set the number of Max  components to 8 this gives us a much  improved for download speed so if you're  interested in using either of these  flags to improve performance check out  the article linked in the description  below and don't forget to check out the  rest of cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts
 

